    "explanation": "This is a wrong-kerb pickup, not a no-show, and the money follows that finding.\n\nTo the rider: the S$4.00 cancellation fee is refunded to you in full. You were waiting where the app pinned you — your own device track holds you between about 8 and 23 metres of the Lobby L pin for the entire wait, well inside the 150-metre rider-presence radius for a large venue — and the charge cannot stand. To the driver: you are not being blamed. Your trace shows you holding station about 285 metres away at the HarbourFront kerb, outside the 120-metre arrival radius but inside the 400-metre limit POL-NS-03 sets, and the app's map put you there. Because neither of you named a sub-location in the in-app chat (it holds only two system messages) and this venue has four registered pickup points, POL-NS-03(ii) treats this as pin ambiguity rather than fault and gives you a platform-funded goodwill payment of half the fee — S$2.00 — charged to the platform and never taken from the rider. The refund and your goodwill are independent and are not netted.\n\nHow I got there. POL-NS-01, the baseline no-show clause, needs all four of its conditions. Condition (b) fails: no GPS fix of yours falls inside the 120-metre arrival radius (qualifying_fix_count 0). Condition (d) fails: the trip was closed with DRIVER_UNABLE_TO_LOCATE, which the clause expressly excludes as the driver's own admission that he could not find the rider. POL-NS-02 would itself refund the fee on the total absence of an in-radius fix, but it yields to POL-NS-03 where the driver was stationary for the required dwell within 400 metres, and that is what the trace shows — a hold of roughly 400 seconds against the 300-second LARGE_VENUE minimum, at a closest recorded approach of 284.95 metres and 0.0 km/h. POL-NS-03 therefore governs: it refunds the S$4.00 and, on limb (ii), awards the S$2.00 goodwill.\n\nOn the other arguments. I accept both sides' reading that POL-NS-01 fails and the case falls to POL-NS-03, and both sides' reading that POL-NS-04 does not apply — the chat log carries no rider coordinating message, so limb (a) never opens and the fault allocation stays with POL-NS-03. POL-NS-05 does not bite either: the 284.95-metre closest approach is inside the 300-metre false-marker line and the stationary fixes read 0.0 km/h, so no false-arrival record arises and the clause's own closing sentence sends this driver, stationary within 300 m but outside the radius, to POL-NS-03. On POL-NS-07, the anti-farming check does not trip (1 no-show termination in 30 days against 187 completed trips, a rate of 0.005, against the thresholds of 4 and 0.08), so nothing is withheld and no POL-NS-09 escalation is engaged; the S$2.00 here is the POL-NS-03(ii) platform goodwill, not the 80 per cent payout, which requires POL-NS-01 to be satisfied in full and it is not. No POL-NS-09 trigger is present: no two admissible artifacts contradict each other on arrival, dwell or rider presence (the trip record's 6.6-minute wait and the trace's ~400-second hold agree), the counterparty counts are 0 on both profiles, rider no_show_disputes_30d is 0, and no deciding artifact is impaired under POL-NS-08 limbs (a) to (d).\n\nOn confidence. The deciding position fact is carried by the GPS trace EV-GPS-00206, which the pre-pass tagged IMPAIRED at weight 0.8 for LOW_SAMPLE_RATE (median 18-second cadence). I do not accept the suggestion that the cap is 1.0: the trip record does not independently place the vehicle, so the deciding fact rests on the 0.8 trace alone. That impairment is schema-level only and fails every limb of POL-NS-08 — the largest interval is 26 seconds against the 120-second gap threshold, there are 52 fixes against the 6-fix threshold, the rider track is present, and the S$4.00 fare reconciles with fee_schedule.resolution_rule (the greater of the LARGE_VENUE S$4.00 and the RYDE_X S$4.00) — so the trace is not impaired for policy purposes and the 284.95-metre figure stands at full value. The 0.8 cap is above the 0.70 escalation floor, so this case is decided today rather than held."
  },
